                <h2>2 AM: The Ad Account Suddenly Gets Banned</h2>
<p>Xiao Lin, head of overseas operations for a utility app, received an email notification from Meta Business Manager at 2 AM: <strong>Your ad account has been disabled due to "suspicious payment activity."</strong></p>
<p>Worse, there was approximately $3,200 remaining in the account balance, with 5 creative variations running A/B tests, and a major promotional campaign scheduled for the next day. If this couldn't be resolved quickly, the losses wouldn't just be the balance—the entire promotion timeline would be derailed.</p>
<p>This scenario isn't uncommon. According to our backend data, <strong>over 40% of overseas teams have experienced account bans during ad campaigns</strong>, with compliance issues accounting for the majority. The problem for many teams is: they only know how to appeal, but not how to systematically investigate the root cause or establish preventive mechanisms.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Problem Analysis: Four Common Reasons for Account Bans</h2>
<p>Before discussing solutions, let's clarify "what the problem is." Ad account bans typically fall into these four categories:</p>
<h3>1. Payment-Related Issues</h3>
<ul>
<li>Credit cards/payment methods flagged as high-risk by the platform</li>
<li>Multiple accounts sharing the same payment method</li>
<li>Abnormal recharge amounts (e.g., large single deposits immediately followed by heavy spending)</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Creative Policy Violations</h3>
<ul>
<li>Landing page content doesn't match ad claims</li>
<li>Images/copy involve exaggerated efficacy or comparative advertising</li>
<li>Violating special regulatory requirements in target markets (e.g., health claims in EU/US, religious sensitivity in Southeast Asia)</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. Abnormal Account Behavior</h3>
<ul>
<li>Frequently modifying ad group structure or budgets</li>
<li>Creating/pausing creatives in large batches within short timeframes</li>
<li>Frequent IP and operation location switches</li>
</ul>
<h3>4. Association Risks</h3>
<ul>
<li>Multiple accounts sharing the same business entity or admin identity</li>
<li>Domain names or brand names similar to already-banned accounts</li>
</ul>
<p>Returning to Xiao Lin's case: after initial investigation, they found the problem was in the <strong>payment环节</strong>: they'd just switched to a new corporate credit card the previous week, but the account holder name had a slight discrepancy (capitalization difference) with the Business Manager registration, triggering the system's "suspicious payment activity" flag.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Resolution Actions: A Three-Step Strategy</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Freeze Current Operations, Gather Evidence</h3>
<p>After an account ban, <strong>definitely avoid repeatedly submitting appeals or frequently modifying information</strong>—this only causes the risk control model to further lock the account. The correct first step is:</p>
<ul>
<li>Screenshot and save the account status page and ban notification email</li>
<li>Export the last 7 days of ad performance data and spending reports</li>
<li>Record account payment history and all currently linked payment methods</li>
<li>Confirm landing page URLs and currently online creative versions</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 2: Precisely Identify the Cause, Submit Targeted Appeal</h3>
<p>Effective appeals aren't about writing the most heartfelt plea—they're about <strong>providing the most precise information</strong>. For Xiao Lin's situation, they focused their appeal on:</p>
<ul>
<li>The exact timing of the payment method change</li>
<li>The correspondence between the two card account holder names</li>
<li>Supporting documentation linking the business license to the payment account</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>48 hours later, the account was unbanned with the full balance restored.</strong></p>
<p>However, for bans caused by creative violations, the appeal focus shifts to: providing landing page backend screenshots proving content compliance, explaining applicable local regulatory considerations, and committing to modify or remove problematic creatives.</p>
<h3>Step 3: Balance Transfer or Refund Processing</h3>
<p>If the appeal fails or the account cannot be recovered, the next step is handling the account balance:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Meta</strong>: Apply for a balance refund through Business Support, typically taking 5-10 business days</li>
<li><strong>Google Ads</strong>: Contact official support to submit a refund request; balance returns to the original payment method</li>
<li><strong>TikTok</strong>: Submit a ticket through Ads Manager explaining the account ban reason and refund request</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Prevention Mechanism: Compliance Audit SOP Checklist</h2>
<p>Problem solved, but more importantly: <strong>don't make the same mistake again</strong>. Here's the daily compliance audit SOP for overseas ad teams, recommended to execute weekly:</p>
<p><strong>Account-Level Checks</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Verify that Business Manager page name matches the business license exactly</li>
<li>Confirm all linked payment method account holder names match completely</li>
<li>Check account security settings: admin emails, operation logs, login alerts</li>
<li>Confirm target投放国家/地区与账户注册主体无冲突</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Creative-Level Checks</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Review against the platform's latest advertising policy documents (Meta Policy, Google Ads Policies) line by line</li>
<li>Pay special attention to consistency between landing pages and ad descriptions</li>
<li>Creatives in sensitive industries (health, finance, gaming) require additional local regulation review</li>
<li>Avoid mixing landing page URLs from different countries in the same ad group</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Operational Behavior Checks</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Avoid significant budget adjustments in a single day (recommended change幅度不超过 20%)</li>
<li>Don't create/pause ads in large batches within short timeframes</li>
<li>Maintain stability in login IPs and operation devices</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>One-Line Summary</h2>
<p>Account bans aren't scary—<strong>what's scary is not knowing why you were banned, being unable to explain the problem during appeals, and having no post-mortem mechanism afterward</strong>. Establishing a compliance audit SOP and detecting problems proactively costs far less than remediation after the fact.</p>
